 ## Background
-Morton order was initially proposed by G. M. Morton in 1966 [S1, S8]. It is a linearization of an $n$-dimensional grid corresponding to the order of leaf nodes encountered during a post-order depth-first traversal of the corresponding $2^n$-tree (quadtree in 2D, octree in 3D) [S8]. The Morton code of a cell is its index in this order, obtained by hierarchically subdividing a Z-shaped space-filling curve [S8]. It is widely used in computer graphics for spatial indexing, particularly in sparse voxel octrees and out-of-core rendering systems to optimize data access patterns [S1, S8][S9, unofficial].
+Morton order was initially proposed by G. M. Morton in 1966 [S1][S8, unofficial]. It is a linearization of an $n$-dimensional grid corresponding to the order of leaf nodes encountered during a post-order depth-first traversal of the corresponding $2^n$-tree (quadtree in 2D, octree in 3D) [S8, unofficial]. The Morton code of a cell is its index in this order, obtained by hierarchically subdividing a Z-shaped space-filling curve [S8, unofficial]. It is widely used in computer graphics for spatial indexing, particularly in sparse voxel octrees and out-of-core rendering systems to optimize data access patterns [S1][S8, unofficial][S9, unofficial].
 
